460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 321 0 R
/T (fuels, there is an urgent need for innovative, sustainable, and eco-friendly energy solutions. This project focuses on the development of a Smart Footstep Power Generation System that utilizes the piezoelectric effect to harness wasted mechanical energy from human locomotion and convert it into usable electrical energy. )
/E (fuels, there is an urgent need for innovative, sustainable, and eco-friendly energy solutions. This project focuses on the development of a Smart Footstep Power Generation System that utilizes the piezoelectric effect to harness wasted mechanical energy from human locomotion and convert it into usable electrical energy. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 321 0 R
/T (The system is designed for high-footfall areas such as railway stations, shopping malls, and smart city infrastructure. It employs piezoelectric sensors embedded beneath walking surfaces that generate an electric charge when subjected to the mechanical stress and pressure of footsteps. To enhance the system's intelligence and utility, an Arduino-based microcontroller is integrated to monitor, regulate, and display the generated voltage and step count in real time. Experimental results from the prototype demonstrate that the system can successfully generate sufficient power to operate low-voltage applications, such as LED lighting and small electronic devices, while remaining cost-effective and maintenance-friendly. The project not only provides a practical solution to energy harvesting but also aligns with global sustainability goals by promoting the use of clean, decentralized, and renewable energy sources. Future scalability could see these systems networked across entire urban landscapes to contribute significantly to green energy generation. )
/E (The system is designed for high-footfall areas such as railway stations, shopping malls, and smart city infrastructure. It employs piezoelectric sensors embedded beneath walking surfaces that generate an electric charge when subjected to the mechanical stress and pressure of footsteps. To enhance the system's intelligence and utility, an Arduino-based microcontroller is integrated to monitor, regulate, and display the generated voltage and step count in real time. Experimental results from the prototype demonstrate that the system can successfully generate sufficient power to operate low-voltage applications, such as LED lighting and small electronic devices, while remaining cost-effective and maintenance-friendly. The project not only provides a practical solution to energy harvesting but also aligns with global sustainability goals by promoting the use of clean, decentralized, and renewable energy sources. Future scalability could see these systems networked across entire urban landscapes to contribute significantly to green energy generation. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
