/E (Rural electrification is potentially a desirable investment in many countries but there has been considerable discussion about the socio-economic and environmental benefits, and the costs of the electrification of these areas in developing countries. The idea that rural electrification in itself substantially contributes to the development of rural and remote areas was already questioned in the eighties. On one hand this was prompted by the experience that most of the expectations were structurally not met, and on the other by a growing discomfort about the below standard performance of many rural power systems in developing countries. Demand forecast was often based on over- optimistic. It is noted that at the time most impact studies were of a descriptive nature but generally speaking there existed no acceptable correspondence between costs and benefits. There is general agreement that the cost and benefits of rural electrification are very difficult to value. But the results of ongoing research based on an advanced assessment of the benefit suggest that the socio-economic benefits of rural electrification are much higher than earlier assumed. For many rural people in the Third World however, electrification of their areas means modernity, progress and, above all, light in the darkness. )
/Pg 52 0 R
