/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 359 0 R
/T (As the historical analysis has shown, the electrification of rural areas should not be assessed as an independent activity, but rather as one of the components of an integrated rural development program. We should not ask ourselves whether electrification of certain areas is feasible, but what we are willing to spend for improvement of living conditions and rural development in general. There is also evidence that an integrated approach to rural development, including electrification, could lead to productivity and business activity synergies, provided that the energy service is reliable. Entrepreneurs in rural areas of the developing world consider unreliable and low service equality as the most important problem for their companies. In many countries, either a national or a provincial power utility was considered appropriate in order to avoid municipal boundaries hampering the development of an economically justified electricity infrastructure, and to achieve an acceptable area coverage and sufficient uniformity of tariffs. Small-scale private rural utilities have seldom proved to be successful \(picture 1\). In quite a few countries co-operatives have been successful, but success seems to be linked with the specific situation and culture in the country, and the opportunities for technical, administrative and managerial support. In many industrialised countries, and some developing countries, separate organisations were made responsible for the implementation of rural electrification programmes )
